SUMMARY = {
 3: ("The animals bring in the biggest harvest the farm has ever had, with the pigs giving directions and Boxer working harder than anyone. "
     "Sundays bring a flag, a Meeting and debates, where Snowball and Napoleon disagree about everything; Snowball forms committees, teaches reading, and boils the commandments down to one short slogan. "
     "Meanwhile Napoleon takes nine puppies away to raise them himself, and Squealer explains why the milk and apples must go to the pigs alone."),
 8: ("The commandments keep changing, Napoleon is praised for everything, and he plays Pilkington and Frederick against each other before selling the timber to Frederick, who pays with fake banknotes. "
     "Frederick's men then attack and blow up the new windmill; the animals drive them off, but many are hurt or killed, and Squealer still calls it a victory. "
     "The pigs find whisky in the farmhouse, and soon the rule against alcohol has quietly been changed."),
 10: ("Years pass, and few animals remember life before the Rebellion; the farm is richer, but only the pigs and dogs live well. "
      "The pigs begin walking on two legs and carrying whips, and the commandments are replaced by a single rule saying, in effect, that the pigs stand above everyone else. "
      "At a dinner with the neighbouring farmers, Napoleon renames the farm Manor Farm, and the animals watching through the window can no longer tell the pigs from the men."),
}

